Minial UI Examples
- when using native UI maya will remember what the last window/layout/etc. created was and by default will assign these
as parents for any further created elements
    - sort of bizarre and bug prone behaviour, try to always assign parents
- the docs for native UI is concise and should be leveraged
    - copy and paste the examples to quickly visualize each component
- groups are basic elements bundled together to achieve common and useful behaviour
- option menu has a different flow, items get added after the menu group is created

Make It Interactive
- most fields have one or more command flags
- commands will fire set code when the condition is met (button press, input changed, etc.)
- commands can take code written as a string as input (useful for super simple single line code
    - there are formatting concerns though and this is super error prone when you can just create and pass func
- be sure to not use () when setting a func in a command
    - this will set the command to the return value of that function rather than the function itself
- when a func is called through command, maya also passes the stat of the field to the func call
    - additional args which then messes up func parameters
    - best to avoid using the additional values being passed
    - catch this by always using *args in your func
- this can mess up func signatures and cause weird behaviour
- from functools import partial
    - allows you to build partial objects
- this is required to pass func to command flag as passing them normally will again fire the func rather than pass it

Deleting a Window
- multiple instances of windows will be created each time your code fires
- common algo for this is to check for any windows with the name of your window and delete them
- this tends to catch 99% of situations

Layouts
- column layout is a vertical box
- row layout is a horizontal box
- CONFUSING and backwards IMO
- layouts can be parented to other layouts to help build complex designs
    - good to try and build some custom layouts (groups) that you like and reuse them
- frame layout is nice looking layout that visually encapsulates a fields or other layouts
    - use the collapsable flag, False by default

Object Oriented Programming
- paradigm that creates objects that bundle common behaviours and properties
- procedural programming (functional programming?)
    - relies on functions to act on arguments being passed in
    - structured as series of steps
- OOP is more flexible when building plug-ins and UIs for maya
- a class is the blueprint of an object, how to create it and what it's default values and behaviours are
- an instance is a living version of a class
- class is the theory, instance is the application
- in python __init__ is the constructor
    - def __init__(self)
- in python 'self' is the equivalent of 'this' in C++/C#
- in python class method definitions always require 'self' to be passed as the first argument
    - when calling a class method on an instance, self is not required to be passed in it is auto passed in
- class variables = static variables
    - in python they are written without the 'self' keyword
    - they are accessible without an instance of the class
- class methods = static methods
    - instance variables are not accessible in these methods as they do not have access to an instance
    - requires the use of a decorator (@classmethod) to make a function a class method
    - instead of 'self' the first arg in a class method is 'cls'
- IMPORTANT NOTE on variables
    - in python, variables can be declared anywhere in the class including methods
    - unlike other languages where variables must be declared in the class scope
    - this means you can do some interesting things with class creation

Inheritance
- extending a class
- subclass of a class
- taking on the variables and methods, adding new functionality of overriding functionality
- 'object' is the base python class
- define the parent class in the class parenthesis
    - class SpecialClass(OriginalClass):
- no special keyword for overriding methods, just write the method in the subclass and put new implementation
- calling base class implementation of a function
    - super().functionName(expected parameters)
        - do not have to pass 'self' in the expected parameters


Importing
- different ways to import
- entire module
    - import module
- specific part
    - from module import class
- specific part and give that part an alias
    - from module import class as cls
- can do import lines inside try except block to catch incompatibilities





